1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,797
For more than a hundred years,

2
00:00:01,797 --> 00:00:03,834
there was one store you could find

3
00:00:03,834 --> 00:00:05,990
in almost every downtown in America.

4
00:00:06,170 --> 00:00:08,875
A bright red sign with gold letters.

5
00:00:09,055 --> 00:00:11,528
Wooden floors that creaked under your feet.

6
00:00:11,708 --> 00:00:15,084
Long counters piled with everything from thread

7
00:00:15,084 --> 00:00:15,946
to goldfish.

8
00:00:16,126 --> 00:00:17,089
And in the back,

9
00:00:17,089 --> 00:00:19,197
a lunch counter where you could get

10
00:00:19,197 --> 00:00:21,304
a grilled cheese and a cherry Coke.

11
00:00:21,484 --> 00:00:22,738
It was Woolworth's.

12
00:00:23,087 --> 00:00:23,831
At its peak,

13
00:00:23,831 --> 00:00:26,557
Woolworth's was one of the biggest retailers

14
00:00:26,557 --> 00:00:27,362
in the world,

15
00:00:27,362 --> 00:00:29,778
with thousands of stores in America and

16
00:00:29,778 --> 00:00:30,831
around the globe.

17
00:00:31,011 --> 00:00:33,739
Its headquarters was the tallest building on

18
00:00:33,739 --> 00:00:34,111
Earth.

19
00:00:34,291 --> 00:00:36,855
Millions of children spent their very first

20
00:00:36,855 --> 00:00:37,809
allowance there.

21
00:00:37,989 --> 00:00:40,474
And then, in nineteen ninety-seven,

22
00:00:40,474 --> 00:00:44,308
the last American Woolworth's five-and-dime closed its

23
00:00:44,308 --> 00:00:44,734
doors.

24
00:00:45,084 --> 00:00:46,327
So what happened?

25
00:00:46,507 --> 00:00:48,922
Let's tell the story of Woolworth's,

26
00:00:48,922 --> 00:00:51,471
from a single store in Pennsylvania to

27
00:00:51,471 --> 00:00:53,483
an empire, what it was like to

28
00:00:53,483 --> 00:00:56,032
shop there in the fifties and sixties,

29
00:00:56,032 --> 00:00:57,709
and how it all came to an

30
00:00:57,709 --> 00:00:57,977
end.

31
00:00:58,327 --> 00:01:01,346
The story begins in eighteen seventy-eight,

32
00:01:01,346 --> 00:01:02,960
in Watertown, New York.

33
00:01:03,140 --> 00:01:06,169
A young clerk named Frank Winfield Woolworth

34
00:01:06,169 --> 00:01:08,097
worked at a dry goods store.

35
00:01:08,277 --> 00:01:10,069
His boss let him set up a

36
00:01:10,069 --> 00:01:11,718
table of odds and ends,

37
00:01:11,718 --> 00:01:14,442
where everything cost just five cents.

38
00:01:14,622 --> 00:01:17,176
It sold out almost immediately.

39
00:01:17,356 --> 00:01:20,340
Woolworth realized he had found something big.

40
00:01:20,690 --> 00:01:22,660
In eighteen seventy-nine,

41
00:01:22,660 --> 00:01:26,126
he borrowed three hundred dollars and opened

42
00:01:26,126 --> 00:01:27,229
his own store,

43
00:01:27,229 --> 00:01:29,828
called the Great Five Cent Store,

44
00:01:29,828 --> 00:01:31,325
in Utica, New York.

45
00:01:31,505 --> 00:01:33,247
It failed within weeks.

46
00:01:33,427 --> 00:01:35,858
But he tried again in Lancaster,

47
00:01:35,858 --> 00:01:37,986
Pennsylvania, and this time,

48
00:01:37,986 --> 00:01:40,114
he added items at ten cents.

49
00:01:40,294 --> 00:01:42,059
The store was a success.

50
00:01:42,239 --> 00:01:44,340
The five-and-dime was born.

51
00:01:44,690 --> 00:01:47,338
Woolworth's idea was revolutionary.

52
00:01:47,518 --> 00:01:49,301
In most stores of that time,

53
00:01:49,301 --> 00:01:51,530
goods were kept behind the counter,

54
00:01:51,530 --> 00:01:53,186
and you had to ask a clerk

55
00:01:53,186 --> 00:01:54,460
to show them to you.

56
00:01:54,640 --> 00:01:56,893
Prices were often bargained over.

57
00:01:57,073 --> 00:01:59,585
At Woolworth's, everything was out on the

58
00:01:59,585 --> 00:02:02,098
counter, where customers could pick it up

59
00:02:02,098 --> 00:02:03,017
and look at it.

60
00:02:03,197 --> 00:02:05,414
And every price was clearly marked.

61
00:02:05,594 --> 00:02:06,860
No haggling.

62
00:02:07,209 --> 00:02:10,507
Woolworth was also a genius at buying.

63
00:02:10,687 --> 00:02:13,290
Because he ordered such huge quantities,

64
00:02:13,290 --> 00:02:16,283
he could demand low prices from manufacturers.

65
00:02:16,463 --> 00:02:19,014
He traveled to Europe to buy Christmas

66
00:02:19,014 --> 00:02:22,237
ornaments directly from glassblowers in Germany,

67
00:02:22,237 --> 00:02:24,990
and he's often credited with making glass

68
00:02:24,990 --> 00:02:27,608
Christmas ornaments popular in America.

69
00:02:27,788 --> 00:02:29,397
By the nineteen hundreds,

70
00:02:29,397 --> 00:02:31,843
his stores sold millions of them every

71
00:02:31,843 --> 00:02:32,165
year.

72
00:02:32,515 --> 00:02:34,570
Woolworth expanded fast.

73
00:02:34,750 --> 00:02:37,275
He opened stores in town after town,

74
00:02:37,275 --> 00:02:40,360
and partnered with relatives and friends who

75
00:02:40,360 --> 00:02:41,833
ran their own chains.

76
00:02:42,013 --> 00:02:43,597
In nineteen twelve,

77
00:02:43,597 --> 00:02:46,017
they merged into one company,

78
00:02:46,017 --> 00:02:46,517
the F.

79
00:02:46,697 --> 00:02:47,638
W.

80
00:02:47,818 --> 00:02:51,660
Woolworth Company, with almost six hundred stores.

81
00:02:51,840 --> 00:02:53,766
It was one of the largest retail

82
00:02:53,766 --> 00:02:55,149
companies in the world.

83
00:02:55,499 --> 00:02:56,914
In nineteen thirteen,

84
00:02:56,914 --> 00:02:59,876
Woolworth opened his new headquarters in New

85
00:02:59,876 --> 00:03:00,550
York City.

86
00:03:00,730 --> 00:03:04,204
The Woolworth Building was fifty-seven stories high,

87
00:03:04,204 --> 00:03:05,674
and for sixteen years,

88
00:03:05,674 --> 00:03:07,945
it was the tallest building in the

89
00:03:07,945 --> 00:03:08,346
world.

90
00:03:08,526 --> 00:03:11,069
People called it the Cathedral of Commerce.

91
00:03:11,249 --> 00:03:13,025
And here's the amazing part.

92
00:03:13,205 --> 00:03:16,184
Woolworth paid for it entirely in cash,

93
00:03:16,184 --> 00:03:19,393
about thirteen and a half million dollars.

94
00:03:19,742 --> 00:03:23,121
Woolworth himself died in nineteen nineteen.

95
00:03:23,301 --> 00:03:24,961
But the company kept growing.

96
00:03:25,141 --> 00:03:26,496
Through the twenties,

97
00:03:26,496 --> 00:03:27,851
thirties and forties,

98
00:03:27,851 --> 00:03:30,754
a Woolworth's opened in nearly every American

99
00:03:30,754 --> 00:03:32,689
town big enough to have a Main

100
00:03:32,689 --> 00:03:33,140
Street.

101
00:03:33,320 --> 00:03:35,123
It became as much a part of

102
00:03:35,123 --> 00:03:37,526
the landscape as the post office and

103
00:03:37,526 --> 00:03:38,127
the bank.

104
00:03:38,476 --> 00:03:39,888
During the Depression,

105
00:03:39,888 --> 00:03:42,389
Woolworth's became even more important.

106
00:03:42,569 --> 00:03:45,243
When families had almost no money,

107
00:03:45,243 --> 00:03:47,917
a store where things cost a nickel

108
00:03:47,917 --> 00:03:49,883
or a dime was a lifeline.

109
00:03:50,063 --> 00:03:53,070
Mothers bought thread to mend old clothes,

110
00:03:53,070 --> 00:03:56,149
cheap kitchenware, and small treats for the

111
00:03:56,149 --> 00:03:56,507
kids.

112
00:03:56,687 --> 00:03:59,068
The company kept growing even in the

113
00:03:59,068 --> 00:04:02,110
hardest years, because people still needed the

114
00:04:02,110 --> 00:04:02,573
basics.

115
00:04:02,923 --> 00:04:04,543
So what was it like to shop

116
00:04:04,543 --> 00:04:06,882
at Woolworth's in the nineteen fifties?

117
00:04:07,062 --> 00:04:09,062
You walked in through glass doors,

118
00:04:09,062 --> 00:04:10,708
and the smell hit you first.

119
00:04:10,888 --> 00:04:12,931
Popcorn, roasted nuts,

120
00:04:12,931 --> 00:04:16,368
perfume, rubber and fresh-cut fabric,

121
00:04:16,368 --> 00:04:18,133
all mixed together.

122
00:04:18,313 --> 00:04:20,298
The floors were wooden and creaky.

123
00:04:20,478 --> 00:04:23,462
Ceiling fans turned slowly overhead.

124
00:04:23,811 --> 00:04:26,453
The counters were long and divided into

125
00:04:26,453 --> 00:04:28,281
sections with glass panels.

126
00:04:28,461 --> 00:04:30,414
There were sewing notions,

127
00:04:30,414 --> 00:04:31,992
with thread, buttons,

128
00:04:31,992 --> 00:04:33,570
zippers and patterns.

129
00:04:33,750 --> 00:04:35,085
Kitchen gadgets.

130
00:04:35,265 --> 00:04:35,869
Hardware.

131
00:04:36,049 --> 00:04:36,699
Toys.

132
00:04:36,879 --> 00:04:38,609
School supplies.

133
00:04:38,789 --> 00:04:40,228
Greeting cards.

134
00:04:40,408 --> 00:04:43,365
Cosmetics, like Evening in Paris perfume in

135
00:04:43,365 --> 00:04:44,808
a little blue bottle.

136
00:04:44,988 --> 00:04:47,450
Hairnets, bobby pins and curlers.

137
00:04:47,799 --> 00:04:50,260
There was a candy counter with dozens

138
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:51,191
of glass bins,

139
00:04:51,191 --> 00:04:53,319
and a clerk who weighed out your

140
00:04:53,319 --> 00:04:55,314
candy on a scale and poured it

141
00:04:55,314 --> 00:04:56,844
into a white paper bag.

142
00:04:57,024 --> 00:05:01,443
Chocolate-covered peanuts, malted milk balls,

143
00:05:01,443 --> 00:05:04,094
orange slices, spice drops,

144
00:05:04,094 --> 00:05:06,451
licorice and nonpareils.

145
00:05:06,631 --> 00:05:08,465
Sold by the quarter pound.

146
00:05:08,815 --> 00:05:10,441
And there was the pet department.

147
00:05:10,621 --> 00:05:12,850
Goldfish swimming in tanks.

148
00:05:13,030 --> 00:05:15,352
Parakeets chirping in cages.

149
00:05:15,532 --> 00:05:17,916
Turtles in little plastic bowls with a

150
00:05:17,916 --> 00:05:19,421
palm tree in the middle.

151
00:05:19,601 --> 00:05:20,495
Hamsters.

152
00:05:20,675 --> 00:05:23,050
Many children got their very first pet

153
00:05:23,050 --> 00:05:25,425
at Woolworth's, and carried it home in

154
00:05:25,425 --> 00:05:27,675
a little cardboard box with holes in

155
00:05:27,675 --> 00:05:28,175
the lid.

156
00:05:28,525 --> 00:05:29,825
And at Christmas,

157
00:05:29,825 --> 00:05:31,660
Woolworth's was magical.

158
00:05:31,840 --> 00:05:34,682
The store was decorated with tinsel,

159
00:05:34,682 --> 00:05:36,182
garland and lights.

160
00:05:36,362 --> 00:05:39,119
There were whole counters of ornaments,

161
00:05:39,119 --> 00:05:41,947
wrapping paper, ribbon and little gifts.

162
00:05:42,127 --> 00:05:44,378
Children with a dollar or two came

163
00:05:44,378 --> 00:05:46,829
in to buy presents for their parents.

164
00:05:47,009 --> 00:05:49,193
A bottle of perfume for Mom,

165
00:05:49,193 --> 00:05:51,690
and a handkerchief or a tie clip

166
00:05:51,690 --> 00:05:52,314
for Dad.

167
00:05:52,664 --> 00:05:54,069
There were records too.

168
00:05:54,249 --> 00:05:56,329
A rack of the latest forty-fives,

169
00:05:56,329 --> 00:05:58,913
and sometimes a listening booth where you

170
00:05:58,913 --> 00:06:01,119
could hear a song before you bought

171
00:06:01,119 --> 00:06:01,308
it.

172
00:06:01,488 --> 00:06:04,274
Woolworth's sold cheap versions of the hits,

173
00:06:04,274 --> 00:06:05,983
recorded by studio singers,

174
00:06:05,983 --> 00:06:08,199
at a lower price than the original.

175
00:06:08,379 --> 00:06:10,914
Many kids bought their very first record

176
00:06:10,914 --> 00:06:13,576
there, and some didn't realize until later

177
00:06:13,576 --> 00:06:15,414
that it wasn't the real band.

178
00:06:15,764 --> 00:06:16,654
In the back,

179
00:06:16,654 --> 00:06:17,988
or along one side,

180
00:06:17,988 --> 00:06:19,619
was the lunch counter.

181
00:06:19,799 --> 00:06:22,665
Woolworth's was one of the biggest restaurant

182
00:06:22,665 --> 00:06:24,002
operators in America.

183
00:06:24,182 --> 00:06:27,186
Its lunch counters served millions of meals

184
00:06:27,186 --> 00:06:27,955
every year.

185
00:06:28,135 --> 00:06:30,260
The menu was simple and cheap.

186
00:06:30,440 --> 00:06:32,967
Grilled cheese, tuna salad,

187
00:06:32,967 --> 00:06:36,056
hot turkey sandwiches with gravy,

188
00:06:36,056 --> 00:06:37,928
BLTs, soups and pie.

189
00:06:38,278 --> 00:06:41,349
Shoppers stopped for a cup of coffee

190
00:06:41,349 --> 00:06:42,969
and a slice of pie.

191
00:06:43,149 --> 00:06:45,645
Office workers ate a quick lunch.

192
00:06:45,825 --> 00:06:48,452
Mothers treated their kids to a banana

193
00:06:48,452 --> 00:06:48,867
split.

194
00:06:49,047 --> 00:06:51,938
Teenagers sat at the counter after school.

195
00:06:52,118 --> 00:06:53,166
For many people,

196
00:06:53,166 --> 00:06:55,526
a meal at the Woolworth's counter is

197
00:06:55,526 --> 00:06:57,820
one of their most vivid memories of

198
00:06:57,820 --> 00:06:58,410
downtown.

199
00:06:58,760 --> 00:07:01,431
And Woolworth's was famous for its desserts.

200
00:07:01,611 --> 00:07:04,014
The banana split was a specialty.

201
00:07:04,194 --> 00:07:05,108
At some stores,

202
00:07:05,108 --> 00:07:07,364
there were balloons over the counter.

203
00:07:07,544 --> 00:07:08,546
You popped one,

204
00:07:08,546 --> 00:07:10,618
and a slip of paper inside told

205
00:07:10,618 --> 00:07:12,890
you what you'd pay for your split,

206
00:07:12,890 --> 00:07:15,095
anywhere from a penny to the full

207
00:07:15,095 --> 00:07:15,496
price.

208
00:07:15,676 --> 00:07:17,012
Kids loved it.

209
00:07:17,361 --> 00:07:20,670
The waitresses were part of the experience.

210
00:07:20,850 --> 00:07:22,635
Many worked there for decades,

211
00:07:22,635 --> 00:07:24,716
and they knew the regulars by name.

212
00:07:24,896 --> 00:07:27,898
They wore uniforms with little white aprons,

213
00:07:27,898 --> 00:07:30,423
and they could balance four plates on

214
00:07:30,423 --> 00:07:30,968
one arm.

215
00:07:31,148 --> 00:07:32,611
They called you honey,

216
00:07:32,611 --> 00:07:35,005
refilled your coffee without asking,

217
00:07:35,005 --> 00:07:37,399
and slipped the kids an extra cherry

218
00:07:37,399 --> 00:07:38,463
on their sundae.

219
00:07:38,812 --> 00:07:41,526
But the Woolworth's lunch counter also has

220
00:07:41,526 --> 00:07:44,304
a very important place in American history.

221
00:07:44,484 --> 00:07:45,895
In much of the South,

222
00:07:45,895 --> 00:07:47,979
lunch counters were segregated.

223
00:07:48,159 --> 00:07:50,573
Black customers could shop in the store,

224
00:07:50,573 --> 00:07:52,505
but they were not allowed to sit

225
00:07:52,505 --> 00:07:53,894
and eat at the counter.

226
00:07:54,243 --> 00:07:55,566
On February first,

227
00:07:55,566 --> 00:07:59,164
nineteen sixty, four young Black college students

228
00:07:59,164 --> 00:08:02,176
sat down at the Woolworth's lunch counter

229
00:08:02,176 --> 00:08:04,379
in Greensboro, North Carolina.

230
00:08:04,559 --> 00:08:07,154
Their names were Ezell Blair Junior,

231
00:08:07,154 --> 00:08:10,759
Franklin McCain, Joseph McNeil and David Richmond.

232
00:08:10,939 --> 00:08:12,889
They politely ordered coffee.

233
00:08:13,069 --> 00:08:14,811
They were refused service.

234
00:08:14,991 --> 00:08:17,230
And they stayed in their seats until

235
00:08:17,230 --> 00:08:18,288
the store closed.

236
00:08:18,638 --> 00:08:19,652
The next day,

237
00:08:19,652 --> 00:08:21,134
more students came.

238
00:08:21,314 --> 00:08:23,670
Within days, there were hundreds.

239
00:08:23,850 --> 00:08:26,537
The sit-ins spread across the South,

240
00:08:26,537 --> 00:08:28,030
to dozens of cities.

241
00:08:28,210 --> 00:08:31,266
By July, the Greensboro Woolworth's served its

242
00:08:31,266 --> 00:08:33,725
first Black customers at the counter.

243
00:08:33,905 --> 00:08:36,498
Today, a section of that counter is

244
00:08:36,498 --> 00:08:40,129
displayed at the Smithsonian's National Museum of

245
00:08:40,129 --> 00:08:42,427
American History in Washington.

246
00:08:42,777 --> 00:08:45,667
Those four students showed incredible courage.

247
00:08:45,847 --> 00:08:47,008
They were heckled,

248
00:08:47,008 --> 00:08:49,459
threatened and had food poured on them

249
00:08:49,459 --> 00:08:50,491
by angry crowds.

250
00:08:50,671 --> 00:08:53,291
But they stayed calm and kept coming

251
00:08:53,291 --> 00:08:54,746
back, day after day.

252
00:08:54,926 --> 00:08:57,380
The sit-ins became one of the turning

253
00:08:57,380 --> 00:08:59,767
points of the civil rights movement,

254
00:08:59,767 --> 00:09:02,286
and inspired young people all over the

255
00:09:02,286 --> 00:09:03,878
country to take a stand.

256
00:09:04,228 --> 00:09:06,029
In the fifties and sixties,

257
00:09:06,029 --> 00:09:07,896
Woolworth's was at its peak.

258
00:09:08,076 --> 00:09:09,467
By the late fifties,

259
00:09:09,467 --> 00:09:12,597
it was celebrating its eightieth anniversary,

260
00:09:12,597 --> 00:09:15,031
with more than two thousand stores.

261
00:09:15,211 --> 00:09:17,436
It had expanded to Canada,

262
00:09:17,436 --> 00:09:20,516
Britain, Germany, Mexico and beyond.

263
00:09:20,696 --> 00:09:23,204
In Britain, Woolworths became one of the

264
00:09:23,204 --> 00:09:25,398
most beloved stores in the country.

265
00:09:25,749 --> 00:09:29,069
The five-and-dime name was outdated by then.

266
00:09:29,249 --> 00:09:30,865
Prices had gone up,

267
00:09:30,865 --> 00:09:33,502
and many items cost a dollar or

268
00:09:33,502 --> 00:09:33,928
more.

269
00:09:34,108 --> 00:09:35,605
But the idea was the same.

270
00:09:35,785 --> 00:09:37,495
A little bit of everything,

271
00:09:37,495 --> 00:09:39,965
at prices ordinary people could afford.

272
00:09:40,145 --> 00:09:42,017
And for kids with a quarter in

273
00:09:42,017 --> 00:09:44,201
their pocket, it was still the most

274
00:09:44,201 --> 00:09:45,637
exciting store in town.

275
00:09:45,987 --> 00:09:48,571
Woolworth's also had competitors,

276
00:09:48,571 --> 00:09:51,078
and many towns had more than one

277
00:09:51,078 --> 00:09:52,175
five-and-dime.

278
00:09:52,355 --> 00:09:53,295
There was S.

279
00:09:53,475 --> 00:09:53,858
S.

280
00:09:54,038 --> 00:09:55,571
Kresge, W.

281
00:09:55,751 --> 00:09:55,936
T.

282
00:09:56,116 --> 00:09:57,359
Grant, J.

283
00:09:57,539 --> 00:09:57,817
J.

284
00:09:57,997 --> 00:10:00,598
Newberry, McCrory's, S.

285
00:10:00,778 --> 00:10:01,161
H.

286
00:10:01,341 --> 00:10:03,234
Kress and Ben Franklin.

287
00:10:03,414 --> 00:10:07,558
Kress built beautiful stores with elaborate architecture.

288
00:10:07,738 --> 00:10:10,102
Many Main Streets had two or three

289
00:10:10,102 --> 00:10:11,910
of them on the same block,

290
00:10:11,910 --> 00:10:13,787
competing for every nickel.

291
00:10:14,138 --> 00:10:15,727
In nineteen sixty-two,

292
00:10:15,727 --> 00:10:18,039
Woolworth's tried something new.

293
00:10:18,219 --> 00:10:20,429
It opened a chain of big discount

294
00:10:20,429 --> 00:10:22,572
department stores called Woolco.

295
00:10:22,752 --> 00:10:23,898
The same year,

296
00:10:23,898 --> 00:10:25,699
Kresge's opened Kmart,

297
00:10:25,699 --> 00:10:28,237
and a man in Arkansas named Sam

298
00:10:28,237 --> 00:10:30,856
Walton opened his first Walmart.

299
00:10:31,036 --> 00:10:33,025
It was the beginning of a new

300
00:10:33,025 --> 00:10:34,740
era in American shopping.

301
00:10:35,090 --> 00:10:37,036
That new era was the beginning of

302
00:10:37,036 --> 00:10:38,805
the end for the five-and-dime.

303
00:10:38,985 --> 00:10:41,995
Families moved to the suburbs and shopped

304
00:10:41,995 --> 00:10:44,418
at malls and big discount stores.

305
00:10:44,598 --> 00:10:46,177
Downtowns emptied.

306
00:10:46,357 --> 00:10:49,023
The old Woolworth's stores on Main Street,

307
00:10:49,023 --> 00:10:51,815
with their creaky floors and lunch counters,

308
00:10:51,815 --> 00:10:52,894
were left behind.

309
00:10:53,244 --> 00:10:55,705
Kmart grew much faster than Woolco.

310
00:10:55,885 --> 00:10:57,742
Walmart grew faster still.

311
00:10:57,922 --> 00:11:01,201
Woolco closed its American stores in nineteen

312
00:11:01,201 --> 00:11:02,149
eighty-three.

313
00:11:02,329 --> 00:11:06,020
Meanwhile, the five-and-dimes kept losing money.

314
00:11:06,200 --> 00:11:07,087
One by one,

315
00:11:07,087 --> 00:11:09,826
through the eighties and nineties,

316
00:11:09,826 --> 00:11:11,599
the old stores closed.

317
00:11:11,779 --> 00:11:13,869
Lunch counters were torn out.

318
00:11:14,049 --> 00:11:15,848
The red signs came down.

319
00:11:16,199 --> 00:11:18,485
In July of nineteen ninety-seven,

320
00:11:18,485 --> 00:11:21,326
the company announced that it was closing

321
00:11:21,326 --> 00:11:24,929
its last four hundred American five-and-dime stores.

322
00:11:25,109 --> 00:11:27,603
After one hundred and seventeen years,

323
00:11:27,603 --> 00:11:29,835
Woolworth's was gone from America.

324
00:11:30,015 --> 00:11:32,708
Many towns held farewell gatherings.

325
00:11:32,888 --> 00:11:35,984
Longtime customers came for one last grilled

326
00:11:35,984 --> 00:11:37,532
cheese at the counter.

327
00:11:37,882 --> 00:11:39,542
But here's a surprise.

328
00:11:39,722 --> 00:11:41,800
The company didn't disappear.

329
00:11:41,980 --> 00:11:43,955
It had owned a shoe store chain

330
00:11:43,955 --> 00:11:46,949
called Foot Locker since nineteen seventy-four.

331
00:11:47,129 --> 00:11:48,797
Foot Locker was growing,

332
00:11:48,797 --> 00:11:51,576
while the five-and-dimes were shrinking.

333
00:11:51,756 --> 00:11:54,844
So the company focused on athletic shoes.

334
00:11:55,024 --> 00:11:56,885
In nineteen ninety-eight,

335
00:11:56,885 --> 00:11:59,118
it dropped the Woolworth name,

336
00:11:59,118 --> 00:12:00,904
and in two thousand one,

337
00:12:00,904 --> 00:12:02,988
it took the name of its most

338
00:12:02,988 --> 00:12:04,254
successful chain.

339
00:12:04,434 --> 00:12:07,152
Today, the company that was Woolworth's is

340
00:12:07,152 --> 00:12:08,381
called Foot Locker.

341
00:12:08,732 --> 00:12:11,668
The Woolworth Building still stands in Lower

342
00:12:11,668 --> 00:12:14,404
Manhattan, more than a hundred years old,

343
00:12:14,404 --> 00:12:17,074
one of the most beautiful skyscrapers in

344
00:12:17,074 --> 00:12:17,741
the world.

345
00:12:17,921 --> 00:12:20,228
The old store in Greensboro is now

346
00:12:20,228 --> 00:12:23,552
the International Civil Rights Center and Museum.

347
00:12:23,732 --> 00:12:25,328
And in many small towns,

348
00:12:25,328 --> 00:12:27,789
you can still see the old Woolworth's

349
00:12:27,789 --> 00:12:30,183
building, with its name faded on the

350
00:12:30,183 --> 00:12:30,582
brick.

351
00:12:30,932 --> 00:12:32,612
For millions of Americans,

352
00:12:32,612 --> 00:12:34,809
Woolworth's was more than a store.

353
00:12:34,989 --> 00:12:37,115
It was where you bought your first

354
00:12:37,115 --> 00:12:38,679
pet, your first lipstick,

355
00:12:38,679 --> 00:12:41,555
your mother's birthday present and your school

356
00:12:41,555 --> 00:12:42,118
supplies.

357
00:12:42,298 --> 00:12:43,629
It was where you sat at the

358
00:12:43,629 --> 00:12:45,107
counter with your grandmother.

359
00:12:45,287 --> 00:12:46,825
It was where you learned that a

360
00:12:46,825 --> 00:12:48,561
dime could buy something wonderful.

361
00:12:48,912 --> 00:12:51,139
It was also a place where history

362
00:12:51,139 --> 00:12:53,433
was made, where four young men sat

363
00:12:53,433 --> 00:12:55,390
down and changed the country.

364
00:12:55,570 --> 00:12:58,020
Few stores in America can say that.

365
00:12:58,200 --> 00:12:59,396
Woolworth's can.

366
00:12:59,746 --> 00:13:01,463
If you remember Woolworth's,

367
00:13:01,463 --> 00:13:03,672
you remember the creak of the floor,

368
00:13:03,672 --> 00:13:04,960
the smell of popcorn,

369
00:13:04,960 --> 00:13:06,984
the goldfish in the tanks and the

370
00:13:06,984 --> 00:13:08,825
grilled cheese at the counter.

371
00:13:09,005 --> 00:13:09,991
It's gone now.

372
00:13:10,171 --> 00:13:11,679
But for a hundred years,

373
00:13:11,679 --> 00:13:13,564
it was a little piece of every

374
00:13:13,564 --> 00:13:14,444
American town.

375
00:13:14,794 --> 00:13:16,663
So tell us in the comments.

376
00:13:16,843 --> 00:13:19,501
What do you remember buying at Woolworth's?

377
00:13:19,681 --> 00:13:21,188
Did you ever eat at the lunch

378
00:13:21,188 --> 00:13:22,642
counter, or get a pet there?

379
00:13:22,822 --> 00:13:24,355
We read every comment.

380
00:13:24,535 --> 00:13:27,150
Next time, we're hitting the road on

381
00:13:27,150 --> 00:13:29,910
Route sixty-six in nineteen fifty-six.

382
00:13:30,090 --> 00:13:31,830
Thank you for spending this time with

383
00:13:31,830 --> 00:13:31,971
me.
